  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "How many different four-digit numbers can be formed by rearranging the four digits in \\(2004?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(6\\)",
    "C": "\\(16\\)",
    "D": "\\(24\\)",
    "E": "\\(81\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "A four-digit number cannot begin with \\(0\\), so its first digit must be \\(2\\) or \\(4\\). After choosing that digit, there are three possible positions for the other nonzero digit. The two identical zeros fill the remaining positions in only one way.\n\nEvery valid number has exactly one such first digit and one position for the other nonzero digit, so these choices include every valid number exactly once. Therefore, the count is\n\\[\n2\\cdot3=\\boxed{6},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
